Certainly! To implement a more secure authentication system for your Jenkins instance using OAuth 2.0 and handle token refreshes, you need to ensure that you can automatically refresh the access token when it expires. Here's how you can achieve this:

### Steps to Handle Token Refreshes

1. **Obtain Initial Access Token**: When you first authenticate, obtain an access token and a refresh token.
2. **Check Token Expiry**: Before using the access token, check if it has expired.
3. **Refresh Token**: If the access token has expired, use the refresh token to obtain a new access token.

### Example Implementation

Below is an example implementation in Groovy that demonstrates how to handle token refreshes:

```groovy
import jenkins.security.ApiTokenProperty
import jenkins.model.Jenkins
import com.github.scribejava.core.builder.ServiceBuilder
import com.github.scribejava.core.model.OAuth2AccessToken
import com.github.scribejava.core.oauth.OAuth20Service

// Get the Jenkins instance
Jenkins jenkins = Jenkins.getInstance()

// OAuth 2.0 configuration
String clientId = "my_client_id"
String clientSecret = "my_client_secret"
String authorizationUrl = "https://example.com/auth"
String tokenUrl = "https://example.com/token"

// Create OAuth 2.0 service
OAuth20Service service = new ServiceBuilder(clientId)
    .apiSecret(clientSecret)
    .defaultScope("read write")
    .build(new CustomOAuth2Api())

// Function to get access token
OAuth2AccessToken getAccessToken(OAuth20Service service) {
    OAuth2AccessToken accessToken = service.getAccessToken()
    if (accessToken != null && !accessToken.isExpired()) {
        return accessToken
    } else {
        // Refresh token if expired
        OAuth2AccessToken refreshedToken = service.refreshAccessToken(accessToken)
        if (refreshedToken != null) {
            return refreshedToken
        } else {
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to refresh access token")
        }
    }
}

// Custom OAuth2Api class
class CustomOAuth2Api extends AbstractOAuth2Api {
    @Override
    String getAuthorizationBaseUrl() {
        return authorizationUrl
    }

    @Override
    String getAccessTokenEndpoint() {
        return tokenUrl
    }
}

// Main logic
try {
    OAuth2AccessToken accessToken = getAccessToken(service)
    println "Access Token: ${accessToken.getToken()}"

    // Use the access token for API calls
    // ...

} catch (Exception e) {
    println "Error: ${e.getMessage()}"
}
