#### Step 2: Define the Auto-Scaling Group with Mixed Instances Policy

Next, define the auto-scaling group and specify the mixed instances policy to include both on-demand and spot instances.

```hcl
resource "aws_autoscaling_group" "example" {
  name                 = "example-autoscaling-group"
  max_size             = 5
  min_size             = 1
  desired_capacity     = 3
  vpc_zone_identifier  = ["subnet-12345678", "subnet-23456789"]  # Replace with your subnet IDs

  tag {
    key                 = "Name"
    value               = "example-autoscaling-group"
    propagate_at_launch = true
  }

  mixed_instances_policy {
    launch_template {
      launch_template_specification {
        id = aws_launch_template.example.id
        version = "$Latest"
      }

      overrides {
        instance_type = "t2.micro"
        weighted_capacity = 1
      }
    }

    instances_distribution {
      on_demand_base_capacity = 1
      on_demand_percentage_above_base_capacity = 50
      spot_allocation_strategy = "capacity-optimized"
      spot_max_price = "0.01"  # Set the maximum price you are willing to pay for spot instances
    }
  }

  metric {
    name = "CPUUtilization"
    statistic = "Average"
    unit = "Percent"
    period = "300"
    threshold = "50"
    comparison_operator = "GreaterThanThreshold"
    evaluation_periods = "2"
  }
}
```

### Explanation of the Configuration

- **Launch Template**: Defines the common settings for all instances in the auto-scaling group.
- **Mixed Instances Policy**: Specifies how to distribute on-demand and spot instances.
  - `on_demand_base_capacity`: The minimum number of on-demand instances to maintain.
  - `on_demand_percentage_above_base_capacity`: The percentage of additional capacity to be on-demand instances.
  - `spot_allocation_strategy`: The strategy for allocating spot instances (e.g., `capacity-optimized`).
  - `spot_max_price`: The maximum price you are willing to pay for spot instances.

### Step 3: Monitor and Adjust

Use CloudWatch to monitor CPU usage and other metrics. Adjust your auto-scaling policies and instance types based on the observed behavior.

#### Example CloudWatch Metrics

```hcl
resource "aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm" "cpu_utilization" {
  alarm_name          = "example-cpu-utilization-alarm"
  comparison_operator = "GreaterThanThreshold"
  evaluation_periods  = "2"
  metric_name         = "CPUUtilization"
  namespace           = "AWS/EC2"
  period              = "300"
  statistic           = "Average"
  threshold           = "50"
  alarm_description   = "Alarm if CPU utilization exceeds 50% for 2 consecutive periods of 5 minutes"
  alarm_actions       = [aws_sns_topic.example.arn]

  dimensions = {
    AutoScalingGroupName = aws_autoscaling_group.example.name
  }
}

resource "aws_sns_topic" "example" {
  name = "example-topic"
}
```

### Conclusion

By using both on-demand and spot instances in the same auto-scaling group, you can achieve a balance between cost savings and reliability. The mixed instances policy allows you to specify how many on-demand instances to keep running and how to allocate spot instances based on your budget and availability requirements. If you have any specific questions or need further assistance, feel free to ask!

